[
 {
  "id": "F1",
  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "line": 267,
  "title": "The growth diagnostic's clock can fail cleanup and is read on every child exit",
  "detail": "bornAt = birthClock() runs before discovery on every child exit, including invocations with nothing left to claim. On Linux that reads /proc/self/auxv and /proc/uptime each time, and if either is unreadable or AT_CLKTCK is missing it throws. stopMarkedProcesses then fails before any scan: runProcess rejects with ProcessTerminationError and the scope latches the error, so later commands are refused. Reproduction (Codex): a Linux-backend fixture with valid ancestry and empty discovery but auxv or uptime unavailable; `exit 0` rejected, and with readable fixtures it succeeded. A diagnostic must not decide whether cleanup succeeds ('Fail closed, precisely'). Fix: read the clock only when a member is first claimed. If it can't be read, report growth as unknown and keep cleaning up. Add a test with the clock failing and nothing to claim, and one with a member to claim."
 },
 {
  "id": "F2",
  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "line": 278,
  "title": "Growth comparison ignores the clocks' precision",
  "detail": "On macOS the cleanup clock is Date.now() * 1000 (millisecond precision), while process birth is in microseconds, so a member born earlier in the same millisecond compares as newer. Reproduction (Codex): an owned sleep spawned before sampling had birth 1792169203:23704 against a cleanup clock of 1792169203023000. On Linux, /proc/uptime (10.03) times 100 Hz gives 1002.9999999999999, so starttime 1003 from the same tick counts as growth. Fix: compare conservatively at the coarser precision (macOS: birth > clock + 1000 \u00b5s; Linux: birth > floor(uptime * hz) + 1 tick, computed in integers). Add both boundary cases to the growth test.each."
 }
]
